-- title:
--   $(\mathbf{Q}/\mathbf{Z})\times\mathbf{Q}$ with the product topology is neither compactly generated nor sequential
-- statement:
--   A product of two sequential (hence compactly generated) spaces whose product topology is neither compactly generated nor sequential.
--
--   Let $\mathbf{Q}$ carry the subspace topology of the Euclidean topology of $\mathbf{R}$, and let $X'=\mathbf{Q}/\mathbf{Z}$ be the quotient space obtained from $\mathbf{Q}$ by identifying the subset of integers $\mathbf{Z}\subset\mathbf{Q}$ to a single point (not the quotient group), with the quotient topology. Recall that a space is *sequential* if every sequentially closed subset is closed, and *compactly generated* if a subset is closed as soon as its preimage under every continuous map from a compact Hausdorff space is closed. Both $\mathbf{Q}$ (a metric space) and $X'$ (a quotient of a metric space) are sequential, and every sequential space is compactly generated.
--
--   **Statement.** The product $X'\times_0\mathbf{Q}$ with the product topology is
--
--   1. not compactly generated;
--   2. not sequential.
--
--   Hence neither the class of compactly generated spaces nor the class of sequential spaces is closed under binary products with the product topology: the product of $X'$ and $\mathbf{Q}$ in the category $\mathbf{CG}$ of compactly generated spaces is the strictly finer space $k(X'\times_0\mathbf{Q})$. In the paper this is deduced from the fact that $(\mathbf{Q}\to\mathbf{Q}/\mathbf{Z})\times\mathrm{id}_{\mathbf{Q}}$ is not a quotient map for the product topologies, whereas products of quotient maps are quotient maps for the $k$-ified products; it is the reason why first countability is assumed in the statement that the product of two first-countable spaces is compactly generated.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** $X'$ is `Quot r` for the relation `r x y := ∃ m n : ℤ, x = m ∧ y = n` on `ℚ` (the equivalence relation generated by `r` identifies two rationals exactly when they are equal or both integers), with Mathlib's quotient topology; the product type carries the product topology. `CompactlyGeneratedSpace` is Mathlib's notion, here with test spaces the compact Hausdorff spaces in `Type` (universe $0$).
-- source:
--   A. M. Masullo, The Music of the Primes, draft of 20 November 2026, https://math216.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/the-music-of-the-primes.pdf, p. 844, Example E.1.2.24 (with Remark E.1.2.23), deduced there from Lemma E.1.2.17 and Example E.1.2.18; for the underlying example the paper cites R. Brown, Topology and Groupoids: a Geometric Account of General Topology, Homotopy Types and the Fundamental Groupoid, Createspace (2006), Ch. 4, Example 4

import Mathlib

open Topology TopologicalSpace

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace CGSpace

theorem not_compactlyGeneratedSpace_ratModInt_prod_rat :
    ¬ CompactlyGeneratedSpace (Quot (fun x y : ℚ => ∃ m n : ℤ, x = m ∧ y = n) × ℚ) ∧
      ¬ SequentialSpace (Quot (fun x y : ℚ => ∃ m n : ℤ, x = m ∧ y = n) × ℚ) := by sorry

end CGSpace
